{
  "id": "al15",
  "title": "The Teletype",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "al15-l1",
      "title": "ASCII",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "With a serial card, the Altair could talk to a Teletype: a typewriter-like terminal that prints on paper. Characters travel as ASCII codes, one byte each: 'A' is 65 (101 octal), 'B' 66, '0' is 48, a space 32.",
        "OUT 001 (323, 001) sends A to the Teletype, which prints the character.",
        "Code 10 starts a new line (line feed)."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "076   ; 000: MVI A",
          "110   ;      72",
          "323   ; 002: OUT 001",
          "001   ;      port 001",
          "076   ; 004: MVI A",
          "111   ;      73",
          "323   ; 006: OUT 001",
          "001   ;      port 001",
          "076   ; 010: MVI A",
          "012   ;      10",
          "323   ; 012: OUT 001",
          "001   ;      port 001",
          "166   ; 014: HLT"
        ],
        "output": [
          "HI"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "al15-l1-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What is ASCII?",
          "options": [
            "A code that gives every character a number",
            "A programming language",
            "A kind of Teletype",
            "A processor"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "American Standard Code for Information Interchange."
        },
        {
          "id": "al15-l1-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What is the ASCII code of 'A'?",
          "options": [
            "65",
            "1",
            "97",
            "41"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "101 in octal."
        },
        {
          "id": "al15-l1-e3",
          "type": "switches",
          "prompt": "Set the switches to the ASCII code of 'C'.",
          "answer": 67,
          "explanation": "65 + 2 = 67 = 01 000 011."
        },
        {
          "id": "al15-l1-e4",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does the Teletype print?",
          "code": [
            "076   ; 000: MVI A",
            "117   ;      79",
            "323   ; 002: OUT 001",
            "001   ;      port 001",
            "076   ; 004: MVI A",
            "113   ;      75",
            "323   ; 006: OUT 001",
            "001   ;      port 001",
            "166   ; 010: HLT"
          ],
          "options": [
            "OK",
            "79 75",
            "75 79",
            "nothing"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "79 is 'O', 75 is 'K'."
        },
        {
          "id": "al15-l1-e5",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Send A to the Teletype.",
          "code": [
            "323   ; OUT",
            "___   ; port"
          ],
          "options": [
            "001",
            "377",
            "000",
            "323"
          ],
          "answer": "001",
          "explanation": "Port 001 is the Teletype, port 377 the lights."
        },
        {
          "id": "al15-l1-e6",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Which code starts a new line?",
          "options": [
            "10",
            "13 only",
            "32",
            "0"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Line feed. Real Teletypes also needed 13 (carriage return)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "al15-l2",
      "title": "Printing a Message",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "A message is a row of ASCII bytes in memory, ending with a 0. A print loop points HL at it, loads each byte, stops at the 0, and sends the rest to OUT 001.",
        "ORA A (267) sets the zero flag when A is 0, without changing A: the perfect test for the end.",
        "This is exactly how BASIC and every later system printed text: a loop over bytes."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "041   ; 000: LXI H msg",
          "017   ;      address 017 (low byte)",
          "000   ;      (high byte)",
          "176   ; 003: MOV A,M   ← loop",
          "267   ; 004: ORA A",
          "312   ; 005: JZ done",
          "016   ;      address 016 (low byte)",
          "000   ;      (high byte)",
          "323   ; 010: OUT 001",
          "001   ;      port 001",
          "043   ; 012: INX H",
          "303   ; 013: JMP loop",
          "003   ;      address 003 (low byte)",
          "000   ;      (high byte)",
          "166   ; 016: HLT   ← done",
          "101   ; 017: 'A'",
          "114   ; 020: 'L'",
          "124   ; 021: 'T'",
          "101   ; 022: 'A'",
          "111   ; 023: 'I'",
          "122   ; 024: 'R'",
          "012   ; 025: line break",
          "000   ; 026: 0: end of the text"
        ],
        "output": [
          "ALTAIR"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "al15-l2-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "How does the print loop know where the message ends?",
          "options": [
            "It ends with a 0 byte",
            "It counts to 8",
            "HLT",
            "The Teletype tells it"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "A zero-terminated string, like in C."
        },
        {
          "id": "al15-l2-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does ORA A do here?",
          "options": [
            "Sets the zero flag if A is 0, without changing A",
            "Clears A",
            "Doubles A",
            "Prints A"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "A OR A = A."
        },
        {
          "id": "al15-l2-e3",
          "type": "switches",
          "prompt": "Set the switches to ORA A (2 6 7).",
          "answer": 183,
          "explanation": "10 110 111."
        },
        {
          "id": "al15-l2-e4",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does the Teletype print?",
          "code": [
            "041   ; 000: LXI H m",
            "017   ;      address 017 (low byte)",
            "000   ;      (high byte)",
            "176   ; 003: MOV A,M   ← loop",
            "267   ; 004: ORA A",
            "312   ; 005: JZ done",
            "016   ;      address 016 (low byte)",
            "000   ;      (high byte)",
            "323   ; 010: OUT 001",
            "001   ;      port 001",
            "043   ; 012: INX H",
            "303   ; 013: JMP loop",
            "003   ;      address 003 (low byte)",
            "000   ;      (high byte)",
            "166   ; 016: HLT   ← done",
            "117   ; 017: 'O'",
            "113   ; 020: 'K'",
            "000   ; 021: 0: end of the text"
          ],
          "options": [
            "OK",
            "OK0",
            "79 75 0",
            "nothing"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The loop stops at the 0."
        },
        {
          "id": "al15-l2-e5",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Why is the message after HLT?",
          "options": [
            "So its bytes are never run as instructions",
            "Messages must be at the end",
            "OUT needs it",
            "No reason"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "'A' = 101 would be read as MOV B,C!"
        },
        {
          "id": "al15-l2-e6",
          "type": "order_lines",
          "prompt": "Put the print loop in order.",
          "lines": [
            "176   ; loop: MOV A,M",
            "267   ; ORA A",
            "312   ; JZ done",
            "323   ; OUT",
            "001   ; port 001",
            "043   ; INX H",
            "303   ; JMP loop"
          ],
          "answer": [
            "176   ; loop: MOV A,M",
            "267   ; ORA A",
            "312   ; JZ done",
            "323   ; OUT",
            "001   ; port 001",
            "043   ; INX H",
            "303   ; JMP loop"
          ],
          "explanation": "Load, test for 0, print, next byte, again. (The jump addresses are left out.)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "al15-l3",
      "title": "Altair BASIC",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "In early 1975, Bill Gates and Paul Allen wrote a BASIC for the Altair without ever having seen one, using an emulator on a big computer. Allen flew to MITS with it on paper tape and loaded it for the first time on the real machine. It worked.",
        "Its first question was MEMORY SIZE? Then it printed OK and waited for a BASIC program.",
        "A print routine, CALLed for every message, is the start of everything that came after."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "061   ; 000: LXI SP",
          "000   ;      address 400 (low byte)",
          "001   ;      (high byte)",
          "041   ; 003: LXI H m1",
          "034   ;      address 034 (low byte)",
          "000   ;      (high byte)",
          "315   ; 006: CALL print",
          "020   ;      address 020 (low byte)",
          "000   ;      (high byte)",
          "041   ; 011: LXI H m2",
          "052   ;      address 052 (low byte)",
          "000   ;      (high byte)",
          "315   ; 014: CALL print",
          "020   ;      address 020 (low byte)",
          "000   ;      (high byte)",
          "166   ; 017: HLT",
          "176   ; 020: MOV A,M   ← print",
          "267   ; 021: ORA A",
          "312   ; 022: JZ ret",
          "033   ;      address 033 (low byte)",
          "000   ;      (high byte)",
          "323   ; 025: OUT 001",
          "001   ;      port 001",
          "043   ; 027: INX H",
          "303   ; 030: JMP print",
          "020   ;      address 020 (low byte)",
          "000   ;      (high byte)",
          "311   ; 033: RET   ← ret",
          "115   ; 034: 'M'",
          "105   ; 035: 'E'",
          "115   ; 036: 'M'",
          "117   ; 037: 'O'",
          "122   ; 040: 'R'",
          "131   ; 041: 'Y'",
          "040   ; 042: ' '",
          "123   ; 043: 'S'",
          "111   ; 044: 'I'",
          "132   ; 045: 'Z'",
          "105   ; 046: 'E'",
          "077   ; 047: '?'",
          "012   ; 050: line break",
          "000   ; 051: 0: end of the text",
          "117   ; 052: 'O'",
          "113   ; 053: 'K'",
          "012   ; 054: line break",
          "000   ; 055: 0: end of the text"
        ],
        "output": [
          "MEMORY SIZE?",
          "OK"
        ]
      },
